WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:03.190
Ms Salma solved the garden on graph

2
00:00:03.190 --> 00:00:05.530
paper: three lines and a corner.

3
00:00:06.170 --> 00:00:08.290
Two variables are fine on paper.

4
00:00:09.010 --> 00:00:11.350
Our timetable has hundreds.

5
00:00:12.170 --> 00:00:14.650
Today, a solver does the searching.

6
00:00:15.890 --> 00:00:18.110
Today: your first model in

7
00:00:18.110 --> 00:00:20.570
Python, with Google’s OR-Tools.

8
00:00:21.850 --> 00:00:23.870
OR-Tools is Google’s free

9
00:00:23.870 --> 00:00:24.970
toolkit for optimization.

10
00:00:26.030 --> 00:00:28.530
You describe the problem: the variables,

11
00:00:28.850 --> 00:00:30.770
the constraints and the objective.

12
00:00:31.500 --> 00:00:33.020
The solver does the search.

13
00:00:33.790 --> 00:00:36.570
On a computer or in Colab it handles

14
00:00:36.570 --> 00:00:39.370
thousands of variables; in Darsi,

15
00:00:39.790 --> 00:00:42.270
small models run on your phone, and

16
00:00:42.270 --> 00:00:44.190
the same program runs on both.

17
00:00:45.340 --> 00:00:48.100
First, a model and the variables.

18
00:00:48.860 --> 00:00:50.880
Each bed count is a whole number

19
00:00:50.880 --> 00:00:53.340
from zero to ten, with a name.

20
00:00:54.450 --> 00:00:56.650
Then each limit is one line,

21
00:00:56.970 --> 00:00:58.250
written just as on paper.

22
00:00:58.900 --> 00:01:01.440
And the objective: maximise

23
00:01:01.440 --> 00:01:03.460
thirty x plus forty y.

24
00:01:04.630 --> 00:01:07.290
Finally, a solver solves the model,

25
00:01:07.770 --> 00:01:09.030
and you read the values back.

26
00:01:09.670 --> 00:01:12.970
It prints OPTIMAL, then three and

27
00:01:12.970 --> 00:01:16.050
five: two hundred and ninety, the

28
00:01:16.050 --> 00:01:17.230
same corner as the drawing.

29
00:01:18.430 --> 00:01:21.090
The solver also says how sure it is.

30
00:01:21.700 --> 00:01:24.460
OPTIMAL means the best, proved.

31
00:01:25.190 --> 00:01:27.390
FEASIBLE means a plan that keeps

32
00:01:27.390 --> 00:01:29.650
the rules, maybe not the best.

33
00:01:30.380 --> 00:01:33.420
INFEASIBLE means no plan keeps every

34
00:01:33.420 --> 00:01:35.220
rule, and that’s proved too.

35
00:01:36.400 --> 00:01:36.980
Quick check!

36
00:01:37.770 --> 00:01:40.230
I forget the line for the pupils’ hours.

37
00:01:41.060 --> 00:01:42.700
What will the solver do?

38
00:01:43.920 --> 00:01:45.520
It gives the best plan for the

39
00:01:45.520 --> 00:01:47.600
model it has: eight bean beds,

40
00:01:48.200 --> 00:01:49.280
three hundred and twenty coins.

41
00:01:50.030 --> 00:01:52.470
That plan needs twenty-four hours of

42
00:01:52.470 --> 00:01:53.950
pupils’ work, not eighteen.

43
00:01:54.760 --> 00:01:56.780
The solver obeys the rules you

44
00:01:56.780 --> 00:01:58.600
write, and only those.

45
00:01:59.920 --> 00:02:02.180
So always check the answer.

46
00:02:02.810 --> 00:02:05.330
Put it back into every real limit.

47
00:02:06.000 --> 00:02:07.500
Ask the people who’ll live with it.

48
00:02:08.080 --> 00:02:10.820
And change one number to see if

49
00:02:10.820 --> 00:02:12.480
the answer moves sensibly.

50
00:02:13.610 --> 00:02:15.070
The same goes for fairness.

51
00:02:15.950 --> 00:02:19.050
If every class must get a bed, or no

52
00:02:19.050 --> 00:02:20.970
pupil may work more than two hours,

53
00:02:21.530 --> 00:02:22.810
write it as a constraint.

54
00:02:23.610 --> 00:02:26.090
A solver can’t keep a rule nobody wrote.

55
00:02:27.340 --> 00:02:29.220
In the app, open the modeller

56
00:02:29.220 --> 00:02:30.640
and run the truck load.

57
00:02:31.250 --> 00:02:33.870
Then change the capacity to eleven

58
00:02:33.870 --> 00:02:35.510
hundred and run it again.

59
00:02:36.100 --> 00:02:37.440
Which loads does it take now?

60
00:02:38.710 --> 00:02:39.470
Your turn.

61
00:02:40.090 --> 00:02:41.210
The solver prints

62
00:02:41.210 --> 00:02:42.970
INFEASIBLE for my timetable.

63
00:02:43.530 --> 00:02:44.890
What does that tell me?

64
00:02:46.080 --> 00:02:49.440
No timetable keeps all those rules: find

65
00:02:49.440 --> 00:02:51.720
the clash, and decide which rule gives

66
00:02:51.720 --> 00:02:52.020
way.

67
00:02:52.620 --> 00:02:55.100
Remember: variables, constraints,

68
00:02:55.740 --> 00:02:58.520
objective, then solve and read the values.

69
00:02:59.260 --> 00:03:01.460
The solver obeys only what you write.

70
00:03:02.140 --> 00:03:04.220
And the same Python runs on the

71
00:03:04.220 --> 00:03:06.100
real OR-Tools in Colab.

72
00:03:07.270 --> 00:03:09.950
Try it in class tomorrow: model a small

73
00:03:09.950 --> 00:03:12.370
class problem together, like sharing

74
00:03:12.370 --> 00:03:14.610
thirty textbooks between three classes.

75
00:03:15.310 --> 00:03:17.250
Write the variables and the rules

76
00:03:17.250 --> 00:03:19.230
on the board in plain words.

77
00:03:19.940 --> 00:03:22.100
Then type them into the modeller lab

78
00:03:22.100 --> 00:03:24.080
and run it in front of the class.
